WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:07.116
July, 1787. Thomas Barclay, America's
envoy to the Barbary states,

00:00:07.116 --> 00:00:09.924
is arrested for debt at Bordeaux.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:15.189
From Paris, Jefferson works to free
him, and writes to him in code.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.214
For this code, and one other,
no codebook is known to survive.

00:00:25.214 --> 00:00:30.924
The editors of Jefferson's papers decoded
the letters from a partial reconstruction,

00:00:30.924 --> 00:00:33.235
and put their guesses in brackets.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.121
But Jefferson left the codebook
behind, a word at a time.

00:00:39.121 --> 00:00:43.631
When a coded letter reached him, he
wrote the meaning over the numbers.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.333
William Carmichael, from Spain, 1788.

00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:54.454
Here the attention of our politicians have been
much engaged by the armaments made in Sweden.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.567
Every number with a word above it
is one line of the lost codebook.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:11.789
18 letters give 348 lines of Code
No. 11. Some words have two numbers.

00:01:11.789 --> 00:01:14.682
“Minister” is 396, or 896.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.220
Now the guesses can be tested.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:37.361
Jefferson's letter to Barclay speaks of
opposition from another quarter of the 495.

00:01:37.361 --> 00:01:39.063
The editors read “sea”.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.507
But in Barclay's own letter,
Jefferson wrote larger over 495.

00:01:44.507 --> 00:01:48.793
The editors' guess fails, and
the real word is still open.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:56.281
Code No. 16 was Thomas Pinckney's, in London.

00:01:56.281 --> 00:02:02.654
His letters of 1793 were decoded
between the lines: 262 numbers.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:11.074
Eight years later the same numbers appear
at Tunis. 663 is “the”. 668 is “at”.

00:02:11.074 --> 00:02:16.047
1490 is “to”. William Eaton
was writing in Pinckney's code.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:21.631
The code went from London to
Madrid, Tunis and Algiers,

00:02:21.631 --> 00:02:27.265
and was still in use in 1813. We found
no one who had said it was one code.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:34.295
Andrew Beling followed it through six
more letters and rebuilt 537 numbers,

00:02:34.295 --> 00:02:37.772
testing the table on letters it had not seen.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:45.121
In Algiers, in 1807, Tobias Lear wrote a
passage no one is known to have decoded.

00:02:45.121 --> 00:02:46.440
The table reads it.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:53.033
The English consul here has done all in his power
to [hurt] us (the bracketed word is a guess),

00:02:53.033 --> 00:02:54.838
since our late difference.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.981
Two codebooks, rebuilt from the letters.
One number still keeps its secret.

00:03:03.981 --> 00:03:08.687
Pinckney's 447 regiments, to
embark on a secret expedition.

00:03:08.687 --> 00:03:10.681
447 appears nowhere else.

